  data:  # 0621
    kind: work_item  # 0621
    title: "backfill-training-data-cohort-tags.py hardcodes a 30s git-log subprocess timeout - flaky by construction under runner contention, reads as a false regression"  # 0621
    status: todo_soft  # 0621
    priority: 2  # 0621
    parent:  # 0621
    tags:  # 0621
    - ci_infrastructure  # 0621
    - developer_experience  # 0621
    isbefore: []  # 0621
    duplicate_of: []  # 0621
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 0621
    pr_ref:  # 0621
    description: "Observed live 2026-08-02 on PR #160 (WI-hajif): CI went red with FIVE failures in\ntests/test_backfill_training_data_cohort_tags.py::TestRealV0WindowBoundaries, all\nof them subprocess.TimeoutExpired on:\n\n    git log --pretty=format:%aI %H --reverse -- .agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py\n\nThe failure is NOT in the test. The 30s cap is hardcoded in PRODUCTION code at\nscripts/backfill-training-data-cohort-tags.py:58 (capture_output=True, text=True,\ncwd=repo_root, timeout=30).\n\nMEASURED, same command, same repo, on the dev box: 0.028s wall clock for 52 log\nentries. CI blew a 30s cap on it. That is a ~1000x margin that still failed, so\nthe cause is runner contention rather than a slow query -- and it is the SAME\ncontention that made the PR job sit unclaimed for over 300s in the same run\n(the human confirmed the pipeline was queued, not dead, by sighting it in the\nWoodpecker UI).\n\nWHY THIS MATTERS BEYOND ONE RED RUN. The failure is indistinguishable, from the\nCI summary alone, from a genuine regression in the change under test: five red\ntests in a file the PR did not touch. The PR that hit it happened to be one whose\nauthor could measure the same command locally in under a second and tell the\ndifference. A less lucky PR gets a false regression signal, and the documented\nrecovery (re-run) trains everyone to re-run red CI, which is exactly the habit\nthat lets a real failure through.\n\nFIX SHAPE (not implemented): a wall-clock cap on a git query is the wrong control\nfor contention. Either raise it substantially (a git log over one path has no\nbusiness needing 30s, so the cap is not protecting against a runaway query it\nwould ever legitimately catch), make it env-overridable so CI can set its own,\nor drop the timeout and rely on the job-level timeout that already exists. Prefer\nwhichever keeps a genuinely hung git from wedging the run.\n\nRELATED, do not conflate: INV-rahib Surface 2 (auto-pr's flat 300s\nno-jobs-started timer misreading a contended runner as a dead one) fired in the\nsame run from the same root cause. Different code, same environmental trigger.\n\nREPRO (will not reproduce on an idle box -- that is the point):\n  time git log --pretty=format:'%aI %H' --reverse -- .agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py"  # 0621
    fields: {}  # 0621
- op: discuss  # 0e10
  at: '2026-08-02T05:08:34Z'  # 0e10
  by: agent  # 0e10
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 0e10
  clock: 2  # 0e10
  nonce: '0e10'  # 0e10
  message: "CORRECTION 2026-08-02, after actually reading the CI log. My filing note asserted runner contention as the cause. That was a hypothesis stated as a finding, and the log largely REFUTES it. Ruling out, each with a measurement rather than an argument:\n\n(1) NOT runner starvation. The CI job ran the whole suite in 359.57s; the same suite on the dev box took 572s. CI was FASTER. I had inferred contention from the fact that the PR job sat unclaimed for 300s before starting, which is a different phenomenon (queueing) from a starved process.\n(2) NOT shallow history. .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml has a prepare-git step that unshallows the shared workspace .git, and pytest declares depends_on [build-grammars, prepare-git], so full ancestry is present before the tests run.\n(3) NOT the missing commit-graph. Forced cold locally with git -c core.commitGraph=false: 0.095s. The commit-graph explains a 3.5x difference, not the 300x+ needed.\n(4) NOT massive over-parallelism. The step bounds workers by cgroup CPU quota and the log echoes -n 8.\n\nWHAT IS ESTABLISHED. All five failures landed on ONE xdist worker (gw5) and clustered at 99pct of the run. The test class calls get_commit_timeline fresh in every test with no fixture caching, so a single slow command becomes five failures and burns 150s of that worker on timeouts alone. REPO_ROOT is the real repository (Path(__file__).parent.parent), so this is a genuine full-history path-limited walk over 6444 commits, not a fixture repo. 26 of the 407 selected test files shell out to git, so concurrent git activity exists.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, stated plainly: WHY the command exceeded 30s. The only mechanism still standing is per-read I/O latency on the Woodpecker shared workspace volume, which would inflate an object-read-heavy traversal far more than the CPU-bound tests that dominate the suite -- consistent with a fast overall suite and one starved command. I cannot confirm it from this log, because nothing instruments where the time went.\n\nHOW TO SETTLE IT (the actual next step, before any fix): run the command under GIT_TRACE2_PERF=1 in CI, or add a plain `time git log --reverse -- .agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py` step. If it is fast on a quiet runner and slow on a busy one, the I/O story holds. If it is slow ALWAYS, then something structural about the CI repo state is responsible and my local comparison was never a valid control. Do NOT raise the timeout before knowing which -- raising a cap to hide an unexplained 300x is how a real regression gets normalised.\n\nSECOND, INDEPENDENT DEFECT worth fixing regardless of the above: the five tests re-run the identical uncached query. A class-scoped fixture makes it one invocation instead of five, cuts the worst-case cost from 150s to 30s, and turns a five-red-test failure into a single legible one."  # 0e10
- op: discuss  # b7a1
  at: '2026-08-02T06:45:42Z'  # b7a1
  by: agent  # b7a1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # b7a1
  clock: 3  # b7a1
  nonce: b7a1  # b7a1
  message: "ROOT CAUSE ESTABLISHED 2026-08-02 by the CI probe (WI-fodad instrumentation, pipeline for 80c9f47b). Measured, same step, same command, seconds apart:\n\n    run1: elapsed_ms=1185559  lines=52     <- 19 min 46 s\n    run2: elapsed_ms=19       lines=52\n    post: elapsed_ms=27       lines=52     <- after the full suite\n\nA ~62000x gap between the FIRST invocation and every later one.\n\nIT IS I/O, NOT CPU. run2 is a fresh git process; if the cost were computation\n(delta-chain reconstruction, commit parsing) it would be paid again. It was not.\nSo the first traversal pulls the pack data off disk and the OS page cache serves\neverything afterwards.\n\nWHY THIS COMMAND AND NOTHING ELSE IN THE SUITE. git log --reverse with a\npathspec over 6445 commits must walk the ENTIRE history before emitting its\nfirst line, and the CI checkout has NO commit-graph (fresh clone) and 7 packs,\nso essentially the whole object store is read. It is the only full-history walk\nin the suite, so it alone pays the cold-read cost, alone, every run.\n\nTHE DEFECT RESTATED. The 30s subprocess cap is a bet that an unbounded,\nenvironment-dependent COLD read finishes in 30 seconds. Disk contention (e.g. a\nconcurrent nightly) changes whether the bet loses, but it is not the cause --\nthe cause is betting at all. This retires the contention framing recorded in the\nearlier correction on this item: contention is a modifier, not the mechanism.\n\nTHE FIX DEMONSTRATED ITSELF. The probe ran the same command BEFORE pytest,\noutside any timeout, and absorbed the 1185s cold read. pytest then measured 19ms\nand all five previously-failing tests PASSED (16443 passed, 0 failed, changed\nfiles at 100pct). So: warm the history walk in the prepare-git step, which\nalready exists and already unshallows. One line, no timeout to tune, and it\nfixes the class rather than this instance -- any future test that walks full\nhistory gets the same protection. Optionally ALSO write a commit-graph there\n(git commit-graph write --reachable), which cuts the cold cost structurally\nrather than merely relocating it.\n\nSECOND, INDEPENDENT DEFECT still stands: the five tests re-run the identical\nquery with no fixture caching. With the warm-up in place that is cheap rather\nthan fatal, but it still turns one slow command into five red tests.\n\nINSTRUMENTATION ERRATUM, recorded so the next reader does not trust it: the\nprobe reported \"invocations_started=36 with_recorded_exit=0\" and claimed\nstarted > exited proves processes were KILLED. That inference is WRONG -- git\ntrace2 repeats the full command line only on the `start` event, so the `exit`\nevent can never match a command-line grep. The counter measures my matching\nrule, not process deaths. The elapsed-value awk also printed nothing against a\n359742-line trace. Neither flaw affected the finding, which rests on the three\nwall-clock numbers above."  # b7a1
- op: discuss  # d9b0
  at: '2026-08-02T07:33:02Z'  # d9b0
  by: agent  # d9b0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d9b0
  clock: 4  # d9b0
  nonce: d9b0  # d9b0
  message: "CORRECTION 2026-08-02: THE COMMIT-GRAPH WARM-UP DID NOT WORK, and it made CI materially worse. Reverted.\n\nMEASURED on the next pipeline, with the warm-up in prepare-git:\n    commit_graph=yes  packs=25  loose=2\n    run1: elapsed_ms=852661   (14 min 12 s)\n    run2: elapsed_ms=17\n    post: elapsed_ms=18\n\nSo a commit-graph FILE existed and the first walk still cost 852 s. The\n`timeout 300` bound I added to keep pipelines fast is what defeated the fix:\n`git commit-graph write --changed-paths` must diff every commit to build the\nBloom filters, i.e. it does MORE tree work than the walk it is meant to\naccelerate, so 300 s was never going to be enough. What survives is a partial\nor filterless graph that does not avoid the tree reads.\n\nTHE REGRESSION I INTRODUCED, stated plainly: before this work CI ran in ~359 s\nand simply failed the five tests (each abandoning at its 30 s cap, so the full\nread was never paid). With the probe running UNCONDITIONALLY in the pytest step,\nevery pipeline paid 852-1185 s. I traded a flaky test for a 15-minute pipeline.\n\nTHE MISTAKE IN SHAPE, which is the useful part: the expensive walk is needed by\nONE test that smart-test selects RARELY -- the pipeline that exposed this shows\n\"pytest (2 selected files)\" and did not select it at all -- while my warm-up and\nprobe ran on EVERY pipeline. I put an unconditional cost in front of a rare one.\nCorrect instinct (warm the cache), wrong placement.\n\nREVERTED: the commit-graph build is out of both prepare-git steps and the probe\nis out of the pytest step. CI returns to its previous duration.\n\nACTUAL FIX APPLIED INSTEAD: raise the cap in the production script from 30 s to\n1800 s. This is a backfill/maintenance tool with no latency requirement; the\ncap not job is to stop a genuinely wedged git, not to gamble on disk conditions.\nOnly the PRs that actually select this test pay the cold read, which is the\nright allocation. The class-scoped fixture (already landed) keeps that to ONE\ninvocation rather than five.\n\nWHY THE READ IS SLOW AT ALL -- best current understanding, NOT established:\n25 packs and no usable commit-graph means a path-limited walk does random reads\nscattered across every pack index and pack. 852 s for ~340 MB is ~400 KB/s,\nwhich is consistent with random 4 K reads on a high-latency device rather than\nwith sequential throughput. If /woodpecker sits on spinning rust that is simply\ninherent, and the cheap structural fix would be `git repack -ad` (one pack, one\nindex, better locality) rather than a Bloom-filter build. NOT MEASURED. Do not\nimplement it on the strength of this paragraph."  # d9b0
- op: discuss  # 5917
  at: '2026-08-02T09:31:07Z'  # 5917
  by: agent  # 5917
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5917
  clock: 5  # 5917
  nonce: '5917'  # 5917
  message: "ROOT CAUSE CORRECTED AND FIXED 2026-08-02.\n\nThe previous entry's \"ROOT CAUSE ESTABLISHED\" established the MEASUREMENT, not\nthe cause. It recorded 1,185,559 ms then 19 ms and attributed that to cold pack\nI/O. That attribution is wrong: the workspace volume is a local ext4 SSD\n(/dev/sda2), so a 44,000x gap is not disk latency.\n\nACTUAL CAUSE, from vendor source rather than inference. woodpeckerci/plugin-git\ndefaults partial=true, and its own defaults.go then sets Depth=1 and\nfilter=\"tree:0\". The CI workspace was therefore a PROMISOR repository holding no\ntree objects beyond the single commit it checked out. prepare-git's\ngit fetch --unshallow restores the COMMIT history but INHERITS the configured\nfilter, so the trees stayed missing. A pathspec walk needs a tree from every\ncommit, so roughly 26,000 of them came back from origin one HTTPS round trip at\na time.\n\nEvery prior observation fits this and only this:\n - run2 at 19 ms is a FRESH git process, so nothing in-process was cached, and\n   page-cache warming cannot turn 1,185 s into 19 ms for a 264 MB pack set.\n   Something had to be ARRIVING on run 1.\n - commit-only reads were never slow (dco rev-list, merge-base, the recency\n   window) because commits were present; only trees were absent.\n - the intermittency is workspace reuse across pipelines - a warm workspace\n   already holds the fetched trees.\n - the reverted commit-graph warm-up made CI WORSE because --changed-paths must\n   diff every commit, demanding the identical trees on every pipeline.\n\nWHY EVERY LOCAL REPRODUCTION WAS INVALID, and this is the transferable part: a\nlocally-made shallow clone carries NO filter, so it has all its trees. The CI\nshape was never actually reproduced. Nine hypotheses died against a control that\ncould not exhibit the defect.\n\nFIX (merged, PR #164, dev 6e92792a34). All three .woodpecker workflows pin\nsettings partial=false. Guarded twice because there are two distinct failure\nmodes: tests/test_ci_clone_is_complete.py asserts the declaration statically\n(absent IS the bug, since the plugin default is true) across both of\nWoodpecker's accepted clone spellings; and prepare-git reads\nremote.origin.partialclonefilter at RUN time and fails the pipeline, because no\nstatic check can see the declaration silently ceasing to take effect after a\nplugin bump or a server-side override. Both default-deny per L54, both\nmutation-tested. The 1800 s subprocess cap is reverted to 120 s - raising it was\nworse than the 30 s it replaced, because it let the walk run to completion and\npushed the pytest step past the agent task deadline, so the pipeline was\nCANCELLED (queue: task expired), trading a fast legible red for an\nunattributable infrastructure error.\n\nREFUTED OPTION, recorded so nobody retries it: bounding the walk at the source\n(giving get_commit_timeline a start revision) does NOT fix this. Measured -\n--since=2026-04-01 still leaves 2,766 of 6,450 commits, a 2.3x improvement on a\n44,000x problem. The tool was never the expensive part.\n\nCLOSURE EVIDENCE OUTSTANDING. The PR's own pipeline passed, but a pass is\nconsistent-with rather than proof-of, and this item's statement is about runtime\nbehaviour. Holding open until the dev push pipeline shows prepare-git printing\nthe no-filter line and the pytest step back at its normal duration."  # 5917
- op: discuss  # 1fd6
  at: '2026-08-02T09:46:43Z'  # 1fd6
  by: agent  # 1fd6
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1fd6
  clock: 6  # 1fd6
  nonce: 1fd6  # 1fd6
  message: "POSITIVE CONTROL BUILT AND RUN 2026-08-02 - the control every earlier\nreproduction attempt lacked.\n\nPrior local reproductions were all invalid for one reason: a locally-made\nshallow clone carries NO filter, so it has all its trees and cannot exhibit the\ndefect. Built the real thing instead - cloned this repo from a local bare mirror\nwith the exact flags plugin-git uses, then applied prepare-git's unshallow:\n\n    git clone --no-local --depth=1 --filter=tree:0 file://<mirror> partial\n    git -C partial fetch --unshallow origin\n\nResulting config matches the CI shape exactly:\n    remote.origin.promisor            true\n    remote.origin.partialclonefilter  tree:0\n    commits=6452\n\nCONTROLLED A/B, same repo, same command, same machine, one variable:\n\n  BROKEN SHAPE (tree:0, promisor):\n    cold walk  > 120,000 ms (killed at the 120 s harness cap, still running)\n    packs       5 -> 50 during that walk, plus a mid-run auto-gc repack\n    warm walk   17 ms\n  FIXED SHAPE (partial:false, no filter):\n    cold walk   44 ms, 52 lines\n    packs       1 -> 1   (nothing arrived, because nothing was missing)\n    clone       4,025 ms for the full 320 MB over file://\n\nThree independent confirmations in that data:\n 1. PACK GROWTH IS THE PHYSICAL SIGNATURE. 45 packs materialised during the\n    broken walk; each lazy fetch writes one. The fixed clone's pack count does\n    not move, so the walk is entirely local. This also repairs a hole in the\n    original CI probe, which printed packs=7 BEFORE the walk and never after -\n    pack count was available as evidence all along and I did not capture it.\n 2. THE WARM NUMBER MATCHES CI. 17 ms here against CI's 19 ms second run. Same\n    command, same repo, fully fetched - which is what CI's fast second run was.\n 3. THE LOCAL PROMISOR PAYS NO NETWORK LATENCY, so >120 s is a FLOOR for what\n    CI paid over HTTPS. CI measured 1,185,559 ms.\n\nCI-SIDE CONFIRMATION of the fix, from the dev push pipeline for 6e92792a34:\n    OK clone carries no partial-clone filter (trees are local)\n    repo already complete\nBoth halves: no filter, and --unshallow is now a no-op because the clone is\ncomplete.\n\nSTILL OPEN, and the reason this item is not being closed. What is proven is the\nMECHANISM and the CLONE SHAPE. What has not been observed is the walk's timing\ninside CI after the fix, because no pipeline since the fix has selected the test\nthat performs it - the per-PR manifest excluded it both times. The chain\n\"CI clone is complete\" + \"a complete clone walks in 44 ms cold\" is sound but its\nfinal link is composed, not measured. full-suite runs pytest tests/ on a cron and\nwill produce the actual number; that is the close condition.\n\nSEPARATE DEFECT FOUND WHILE VALIDATING, filed as WI-vilor and already fixed: the\npush pipeline that gave the clone confirmation above SKIPPED pytest entirely\n(fatal: bad object, then \"PR changes no .py files - skipping pytest\", reported\ngreen). The gate failed open on a git error, and its base was CI_PREV_COMMIT_SHA\n- the previous PIPELINE's commit, rewritten by auto-pr's rebase merge and absent\nfrom the clone. That fired on every rebase-merged push."  # 1fd6
